WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.790
Hi, everyone.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.120
My name is Shel, I'm a
data analytics consultant

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.990
based in Nairobi, Kenya.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.350
Today, I'm going to show
you how you can quickly

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.830
make your Shiny apps
mobile responsive.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.730
At the beginning of
the year, I joined

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.650
a team that had
built an amazing app,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.140
but users were finding
it hard to maneuver

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.290
through their phones.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.980
I was required to make
the app mobile responsive

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.810
while making very minimal
edits to the R code.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.690
I took this as a challenge
and within two to three weeks,

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.400
we had the mobile Shiny
app ready and deployed.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.710
Apps are said to be
mobile responsive if they

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:39.010
are optimized to be functional
and aesthetically pleasing

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.850
on devices of different sizes.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.000
For this talk, I
created a demo Shiny  app

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.770
that you can access
via bit.ly/mfano.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.130
You can also access it by
scanning the QR code displayed.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.520
This is the same app, but
with a few differences

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.570
depending on whether you view
it on a laptop or mobile phone.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.060
The first thing you can
do is to set the app such

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.330
that the menu is collapsible.
In Shiny by default

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.260
when collapsible is set to true,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.310
the menu is scalloped

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.220
on the screen size of
940 pixels or less.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:11.050
Here I used CSS and media
queries to tell the Menu icon

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.620
and to reduce the font size of the
tab names and tab background color.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.670
CSS is used to style
HTML elements, and media

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.670
queries enable web page content
to adapt to different screen

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.500
sizes and resolutions.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.000
Think of media queries 
as if-else

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.280
statements. For example,
if the screen size

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.790
is less than 480 pixels
typical of mobile phones,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.940
convert the background color to
pink or the headline font size

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.130
to 8 pixels.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.940
We use breakpoints
with media queries

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.000
to set points where our websites
adjust to the width of devices.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.430
Next, you can choose
to remove some objects

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.710
so that they are not
visible on some devices.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.950
In this example, we
removed the icons

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.360
and introduced a button
on the mobile view.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.050
You can do this by setting
display as none in CSS.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.810
I also used media
queries to edit

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.200
the typography of the
paragraphs for the mobile view.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.230
Shinybrowser,

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.790
a package by Dean Attali, lets
you easily detect a user's web

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.660
browser information in Shiny.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.020
One of the caveats is that
the width and the height

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.389
of the browser window
are only reported once

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.770
when the detect function
is initially called.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.720
So you'd need to refresh the
page to get the new dimensions.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.640
You can use shinybrowser
to reduce

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.040
the font size of different
things, such as plot titles,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.170
axis text, and labels.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.770
You can have a
function that generates

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.320
your graph with an
argument of type of device.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.990
For example, if your
device is mobile,

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.350
we want the title to be of
size 14, otherwise size 18.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.870
Then on the UI, insert
the detect function

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.390
and on the server, define
a reactive element that

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.300
detects the type of device
based on the device's width

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.960
and use this as the
graphs function argument.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.680
And this is what I
did in the Kwanza tab.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.120
The axis text size
in the desktop view

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.630
is a bit larger than that

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.220
in the mobile view. You
can use the same principle

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.200
to change the
orientation of the graph

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.020
as seen on the Tatu
tab on the mobile view,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.090
the chart is flipped.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.940
You can also choose to change
the order in which objects

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.690
appear in each tab, and this
is where CSS Grid and Flexbox

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.720
come into play.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.850
They both define how elements
are displayed on a web page.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.520
Lastly, one trick
that is really helpful

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.150
is inspecting the browser.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.630
You can play around
changing some aspect

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.370
and seeing how things look.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.950
It is also very important
to see how your app looks

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.280
on different devices.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.190
And here you can
ask your friends

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:31.210
to send you a screenshot of
output displayed by myviewport.com

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.770
and add the screen size
dimension in the Devices

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:36.940
section.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.010
Once you are happy
with the result,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.280
copy the rules to your CSS file.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.950
Refresh and rerun.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.310
You can perfectly
see the media queries

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.830
in action on your browser.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.780
And that, my friends,
is how you can

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.060
have a very awesome
and usable, Shiny

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.230
app that can easily be viewed
via a laptop or a mobile phone.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:00.100
Now, disclaimers. Number one,
I worked on this project

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.830
before I learned about
bootstrap and bslib,

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.030
so please check them out too.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.490
I'm not a CSS pro yet, so I believe
I could be more efficient.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.910
And honestly, with CSS, you just
need to keep guessing values

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:16.089
til things work and undo
when things get worse.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:20.980
You can access my code via
bit.ly/mfanocode.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.180
If you are attending the
conference physically

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.810
and you're interested in
hearing more about this project,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.970
please look for me over lunch
hour so that we can chat.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.840
Thank you.

